(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-cardiac-imaging-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiac Imaging Quiz 2 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is transthoracic echocardiography the most common echo procedure?","choices":["It gives better detail of the posterior structures","It requires no operator training","It can be performed at the bedside and is noninvasive","It is the only echo that can use Doppler"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Availability rather than image quality is what makes it the default, and the trade is detail at the back of the heart. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"For which patient is an echocardiographic stress test the appropriate initial choice?","choices":["One who cannot exercise at all","One with a normal resting electrocardiogram who can exercise","One who can exercise but whose resting tracing is abnormal","One with suspected aortic dissection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram cannot be read for ischemia if it is already abnormal, so the imaging has to carry the answer instead. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does echocardiography evaluate?","choices":["The coronary artery calcium score","The distribution of coronary atherosclerotic plaque","The electrical conduction pathways of the heart","Chamber size, ventricular function, valves, shunts and pressures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the functional study of the heart, which is what separates it from computed tomography, and Doppler is what adds flow and velocity to it. Wall thickness and ejection fraction come with the ventricular assessment, and Doppler adds flow and velocity. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which structures does a transesophageal study show in more detail than a transthoracic one?","choices":["The right ventricle and the tricuspid valve","Left atrium, mitral valve, aorta and coronaries","The pericardium alone","The coronary calcium burden"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with the pulmonary artery too. The probe sits directly behind the heart, so the structures that gain are the posterior ones. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which of these is a stated indication for exercise stress testing?","choices":["Cardiac assessment before non-cardiac surgery in a high-risk patient","Acute chest pain before acute coronary syndrome has been excluded","Suspected aortic dissection","Screening an asymptomatic patient with no risk factors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside symptoms suggesting ischemia, known ischemic disease with a change in status, prior revascularization and newly diagnosed heart failure. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What distinguishes a transthoracic from a transesophageal echocardiogram?","choices":["One uses Doppler and the other does not","The transducer is outside the body in one, inside in the other","One uses ionizing radiation while the other does not","One assesses valves and the other assesses chambers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and everything else about the two -- invasiveness, sedation, contraindications, and how much posterior detail you get -- follows from that single difference. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"For which of these is a transesophageal study specifically useful?","choices":["Coronary artery calcium scoring","Valve vegetations","Assessing exercise capacity","Measuring the cardiothoracic ratio"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All are small, posterior, or both, which is precisely where the transthoracic view falls short. Prosthetic valve function, cardiac tumors and left atrial appendage thrombus. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which combination of advantages does vascular ultrasound offer?","choices":["Superior resolution to computed tomography with no operator dependence","Reliable assessment of deep vessels regardless of body habitus","Fast, portable, repeatable, no ionizing radiation","Independence from the angle at which the probe is held"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The combination of no radiation and easy repetition is what makes it the study you can do again tomorrow without cost to the patient. It shows both anatomy and flow. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does a transesophageal study require that a transthoracic one does not?","choices":["Throat anesthesia, sedation and monitoring","Iodinated contrast given intravenously","Electrocardiographic gating of the images","A period of fasting from fluids only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a procedure rather than a scan, which is why it carries a contraindication list and the transthoracic study does not. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph findings are given as indicators of cardiac pathology?","choices":["Coronary artery stenosis and the distribution of plaque","Edema, effusion, cardiomegaly, cephalization and calcification","Ejection fraction and regional wall motion abnormality","Valve pressure gradients and regurgitant volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with tension physiology completing the list. Several are about the lungs rather than the heart, which is the point: the heart announces itself through what it does to everything around it. The calcification may be of the great vessels, the valves or the pericardium, and tension physiology completes the list. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which limitations are described for vascular ultrasound?","choices":["Ionizing radiation dose and contrast nephrotoxicity","Dependence on heart rate and rhythm","Highly operator dependent, limited by body habitus","Inability to show blood flow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcification causes acoustic shadowing, which matters because the vessels most worth imaging are often the ones most calcified. Degraded by bone, air and calcification. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why does calcification degrade a vascular ultrasound image?","choices":["It causes acoustic shadowing","It absorbs the ionizing radiation before it reaches the vessel","It produces a blooming artifact that exaggerates the lesion","It prevents the vein from collapsing under compression"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sound does not pass through, so everything behind the calcium is unassessable rather than merely indistinct. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In which patients is cephalization seen?","choices":["Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","Coronary artery disease without failure","Pericardial calcification","Heart failure and pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both raise pressure in the pulmonary circulation, and the redistribution upward is the radiographic consequence. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is the heart stressed for a nuclear perfusion study in a patient who cannot exercise?","choices":["By transesophageal pacing","By cold pressor testing","The study cannot be performed without exercise","Pharmacologically, with an agent such as adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The stress can be produced by exercise or by drug, and the imaging is the same either way. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Why must a Zenker's diverticulum be identified before a transesophageal study?","choices":["It prevents adequate sedation","The probe risks entering and perforating the pouch","It causes a false positive for vegetations","It makes the patient unable to swallow the local anesthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and because it is a pouch rather than a narrowing, the operator may feel nothing wrong until the damage is done. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why are cardiac medications given before a cardiac computed tomography study?","choices":["To dilate the coronary arteries so contrast reaches the myocardium","To control heart rate, since the moving heart degrades the images","To prevent an allergic reaction to the contrast","To provoke ischemia during the scan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the study is also synchronized to an electrocardiogram so the heart can be examined at chosen points in the cycle. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the echocardiogram looking for after exercise, and why must it be obtained quickly?","choices":["Valve gradients, which take time to develop","Pericardial fluid, which accumulates after exertion","Abnormal left ventricular wall motion induced by exercise","Coronary calcium, which is best seen at high heart rates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The finding is transient, so the patient is asked to dismount quickly and the heart is imaged before it settles. Which resolves as the heart recovers. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does a vascular ultrasound image form?","choices":["Ionizing radiation is absorbed differently by tissues of differing density","A magnetic field aligns protons which then emit radiofrequency signal","An injected tracer is taken up and emits gamma rays","Quartz crystals emit pulses that reflect off tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The returning echoes are what the machine turns into a picture, so anything that stops sound returning leaves a gap in it. The pulses are high frequency, and what returns depends on the density of what they meet. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does nuclear perfusion imaging compare with standard and echocardiographic stress testing?","choices":["It is less accurate but faster","It is equivalent in accuracy but avoids radiation","It assesses anatomy rather than perfusion","Its sensitivity and specificity are superior"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is worth the tracer and the radiation when the simpler tests have not settled the question. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is coronary angiography the gold standard for?","choices":["Coronary artery anatomy and cardiac function","Assessing myocardial viability","Measuring the coronary calcium score","Detecting valve vegetations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is usually performed before any percutaneous or surgical intervention on the coronary arteries. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT among the listed indications for vascular ultrasound?","choices":["Assessment of coronary artery stenosis","Deep vein thrombosis study and vascular access","Carotid evaluation after stroke, transient ischemic attack or myocardial infarction","Abdominal aortic aneurysm evaluation and varicose veins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- this is not one. The coronary arteries are not accessible to surface vascular ultrasound; they are assessed by computed tomography angiography or catheter angiography. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the blooming artifact, and why does it matter?","choices":["Motion during the scan blurs the coronary arteries","Heavy calcification appears larger than it is","Contrast pools in the ventricle and obscures the valve","Radiation scatter degrades the image at the edges"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The artifact exaggerates the very lesion the study was ordered to assess, which is a limitation worth naming rather than a curiosity. So a stenosis looks more severe. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In which position does a patient lie initially for transthoracic echocardiography?","choices":["Supine and flat throughout","Prone","Left lateral decubitus","Standing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It brings the heart toward the chest wall, and the patient is moved through other positions to obtain the different views. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which is an indication for cardiac computed tomography rather than the angiographic study?","choices":["Detecting and excluding coronary stenosis","Assessing exercise capacity","Pre-operative planning before cardiac surgery","Evaluating deep venous thrombosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside calcium deposits, coronary anatomical anomalies and congenital disease, and it is usually done immediately before the angiographic study. Particularly valvular and great vessel procedures. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which aortic conditions is cardiovascular magnetic resonance used to diagnose and monitor?","choices":["Aneurysm, dissection, hematoma, coarctation","The coronary artery calcium burden","Valve vegetations","Deep venous thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four are questions about the wall and the lumen of a large vessel, which is where its detailed anatomy is most useful. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which cardiomyopathy questions is cardiovascular magnetic resonance used to answer?","choices":["The degree of coronary stenosis","Exercise capacity","Left atrial appendage thrombus","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each turns on what the muscle itself is doing, which is where it outperforms the studies that show the arteries. Ischemic against nonischemic, acute myocarditis and cardiac sarcoidosis. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which esophageal conditions contraindicate a transesophageal echocardiogram?","choices":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease","A history of esophageal candidiasis","Recent barium swallow","Stricture, malignancy or bleeding varices"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three mean passing a probe risks perforation or hemorrhage, which is the hazard the contraindication list exists to avoid. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which electrocardiographic changes during an exercise stress test suggest ischemia?","choices":["ST elevation with reciprocal change","ST depression","A prolonged QT interval","Loss of P waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both appear as the heart is worked and are what the continuous monitoring exists to catch. Or the development of premature ventricular contractions. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is a non-contrast cardiac computed tomography particularly useful for?","choices":["Coronary artery calcium scoring","Assessing coronary stenosis","Measuring ejection fraction","Detecting valve vegetations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Calcium is dense enough to be seen without contrast, so the study that needs no contrast is the one that measures it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A properly performed posteroanterior chest radiograph shows the cardiac silhouette occupying 58 percent of the internal thoracic diameter. What does that suggest?","choices":["Cardiomegaly","A normal heart size","Pulmonary hypertension without cardiac enlargement","A pericardial effusion specifically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The silhouette should normally occupy no more than about half the internal thoracic diameter, so this exceeds it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
